    {
    "headline": "Fresher hiring up 1%, mid-level roles fall 2%",
    "essay": "Hiring for freshers with 0–3 years of experience grew only 1% year-on-year, while the 4–7 years category fell 2%. Older cohorts did better: 8–12 years rose 5%, 13–15 years grew 7%, and 16-plus years rose 5%. The pattern shows employers paying for experience and specialization, not entry-level capacity. Naukri describes the market as steady but more selective, with demand driven by specific business and capability needs. For Info Edge, fewer fresher listings means lower volume from the largest candidate pool, even as senior placements hold up.",
    "sources": [1]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Hiring mixed across cities; Delhi-NCR and Mumbai fall 6%",
    "essay": "City-level data shows the same selective pattern. Udaipur grew 19%, Chandigarh 18%, Amritsar and Ludhiana 13% each, and Jodhpur 12%. Hyderabad rose 9%, Lucknow 8%, Chennai 6% and Bengaluru 4%. But Delhi-NCR and Mumbai each fell 6% year-on-year, and Jaipur dropped 4%. Education, teaching and training fell 18%, telecom 12%, and hospitality 11%. The declines in the two largest job markets offset gains in smaller cities. For Info Edge, this geographic shift means recruiter demand is moving away from its highest-volume regions, a drag on overall listing growth.",
