    "headline": "Gold ETFs hit record inflows as futures selling drives 8.5% price drop",
    "essay": "Gold ETFs attracted a record $31 billion in the third quarter, with September inflows of $10 billion lifting holdings by 67 tonnes to an all-time high of 4,256 tonnes, the World Gold Council said. That happened even as gold fell 8.5% in September to $4,176 per ounce. The split comes from futures traders cutting positions: Comex managed-money holdings dropped by 84 tonnes and spread positions by 156 tonnes, according to BMO Capital Markets. Higher yields and a stronger dollar also weighed, with the 10-year Treasury yield up 53 basis points to 5.3% and the dollar index up 2%. U.K.-listed funds led the quarter with $7.5 billion, their best ever, and have pulled in $9.5 billion this year, overtaking Chinese funds. The council says the buying looks like a hedge against fiscal and bond-market risks rather than a reaction to a single shock. For gold, the ETF demand provides a steady bid even as futures selling pressures the price.",
    "sources": [1]
    },
    {
    "headline": "China extends gold buying streak to 23 months with 21-tonne purchase",
